<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Engineer · Architect · Mentor · 17+ years in software"
>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I've worked across large-scale distributed systems, evolving architectures
    and guiding teams through complex technical decisions. I bring clarity to
    complex systems — aligning architecture with long-term business outcomes and
    responsible technology decisions.
  </p>

  <p>
    With years of building and guiding software systems, I focus on judgement
    over hype, trade-offs over trends, and sustainability over speed.
  </p>

  <p>
    I help teams design systems that scale — technically, organisationally, and
    ethically.
  </p>

  <h2>The Long Story</h2>

  <p>
    I started as a Systems Engineer at TCS, building government applications and
    learning the fundamentals. It is here that I learnt not just programming but
    what it means to work in a team, what a good culture looks like. I also
    learnt how to exchange honest feedbacks — a skill whose value I learnt only
    after many years.
  </p>
  <p>
    I worked nearly a decade at Infosys, where I progressed to Technology Lead,
    working on everything from compliance workflows to batch processing systems
    to distributed systems. Most of my time with Infosys was in US, where I got
    an opportunity to work with the client's engineering teams. I learned so
    much more than tech. Good planning, good governance, raising concerns,
    delivering bad news — the ingredients for a recipe to deliver world-class
    software. Along the way, I discovered that the most impactful work wasn't
    writing clever code — it was preventing unnecessary complexity from being
    written in the first place. It was here that I learned the art to say "no".
  </p>

  <p>
    After a short stint as Engineering Manager at xEnabler, I joined Technogise,
    where I found the environment to practice XP, honest trade-offs, and
    technology decisions rooted in business outcomes. At Technogise, I started
    looking beyond software delivery, about creating a lasting culture and
    giving back to the community. Watching the next generation of kids
    surrounded by tech made me think deeply about finding a way to help them
    navigate its complexities, especially when this generation has never seen a
    world without tech.
  </p>

  <h2>What Drives Me</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Making complex technology understandable — so decisions are informed,
      inclusive, and accountable.
    </li>
    <li>
      Ensuring architecture serves real business intent, not just technical
      elegance.
    </li>
    <li>Mentoring engineers to develop judgement, not just execution.</li>
    <li>Sharing knowledge that strengthens the wider engineering community.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why I Write</h2>

  <p>
    The tech industry has a clarity problem. We hide behind jargon, chase trends
    that serve vendors more than users, and treat complexity as a badge of
    honour rather than a cost to be minimised.
  </p>

  <p>
    I write because most engineering advice tells you what to do. I'm more
    interested in why it fails. And I believe non-technical people — business
    leaders, product managers, ordinary users — deserve to understand how
    technology shapes their lives.
  </p>

  <p>Clarity is not just a skill. It is a responsibility.</p>
</BaseLayout>

